// Every Linux syscall number this runtime uses, in one place (#1897).
//
// Before this module the numbers lived in seven `runtime/*/linux/` modules, and
// eight of them were written more than once — `close` four times, `read` three,
// `open`/`write`/`mmap`/`munmap`/`nanosleep`/`clock_gettime` twice each. Every
// copy agreed, and nothing checked that they did: editing one and missing
// another builds clean, links clean, and passes the whole suite while one code
// path issues the wrong syscall on one architecture.
//
// WHY EACH ENTRY IS AN `if` AND NOT A TABLE. A number is meaningless without its
// architecture: 56 is `clone` on x86-64 and `openat` on aarch64. Keying anything
// by the number is therefore wrong by construction — the key is (name, arch),
// which is exactly what the `if` spells out. Writing `perArch(3, 57)` instead
// would be shorter and would hide which of the two belongs to which chip, which
// is the defect this module exists to remove.
//
// SPEC §11.8 is why the choice happens at RUN time rather than compile time:
// "Bit has no arch-conditional compilation, so a program that targets both
// architectures selects the number itself at run time." When that changes, every
// function here collapses to a constant and `onX64` disappears.
//
// THIS MODULE IMPORTS NOTHING, AND MUST NOT. `rtHostTarget` lives in
// `runtime/root/linux/os.bit`, which needs the numbers from here — importing it
// back would be an import cycle (E0044), the same edge `runtime/stw`'s header
// records for chan->gc. So `onX64` below carries its own `asm` immediate, the
// shape `runtime/root/linux/os.bit`'s `rtHostTarget` and
// `runtime/alloc/linux/pages.bit`'s `hostArch` already use.
//
// EVERYTHING IS `@nosplit` because the strictest caller requires it: these are
// reached from the collector's allocation path and from panic handling, where a
// safepoint poll would let a collection run inside the runtime (#1656). Four of
// the seven modules this replaces were already `@nosplit`; the other three are
// satisfied by a stricter callee.
//
// EVERY NUMBER BELOW IS THE KERNEL'S, NOT THIS PROJECT'S: x86-64 from
// arch/x86/entry/syscalls/syscall_64.tbl, aarch64 from the generic table in
// include/uapi/asm-generic/unistd.h. A wrong number is a silent wrong-syscall,
// never a compile error, so none of them may be edited from memory.
//
// FOUR CALLS HAVE NO COMMON SHAPE and are deliberately NOT folded into an `if`:
// x86-64 has `open`, `mkdir`, `unlink`/`rmdir`, `chmod` and `readlink`, while
// aarch64 has none of them and uses the `*at` forms, which take a leading
// directory fd and therefore a DIFFERENT ARGUMENT LIST. Those are exported as
// separate per-arch functions and the caller picks the call shape, not just the
// number — see the bottom of this file.

// 0 on x86-64, 1 on aarch64. One `asm` immediate, emitted inline (§11.6), so it
// is legal inside `@nosplit` — unlike the `hostTarget()` primitive, which lowers
// to a real `call bit_rt_host_target` that §10.3.1's allowlist refuses.
//
// PINNED port-internal: once a one-line wrapper below inlines across a module
// boundary, the surviving object carries a reference to this function, and
// `--freestanding` refuses an unpinned cross-module reference (#1627, §11.9).
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_syscalls_on_x64")
fn onX64(): bool {
  let t = asm {
    arm64 { 0xD2800020 }                    // mov x0, #1
    x64   { 0xB8, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00 }  // mov eax, 0
    result arm64 x0 x64 rax : int
  }
  return t == 0
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// File descriptors
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_syscalls_read")
fn sysRead(): int {
  if (onX64()) { return 0 }
  return 63
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_syscalls_write")
fn sysWrite(): int {
  if (onX64()) { return 1 }
  return 64
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_syscalls_close")
fn sysClose(): int {
  if (onX64()) { return 3 }
  return 57
}

